Boot-arg metal_ubwc_fb=1: CoreAnimation's framebuffer surfaces drawn compressed (UBWC), 2026-11-16, darwin-lisa session 13.
Applies after HakiosMetalCA-ubwc-rt.patch; the core's hakios-metal-y-ubwc-fetch.patch goes with it.

On this chip under freedreno (tools/glfill on pmOS, 1080x2400 RGBA8, 550 MHz, GPU timer) a full-screen blended draw
costs 0.38 ms into a compressed target and 1.45 ms into an uncompressed one; opaque 0.30 against 0.67. Here every layer
of the window server's frame is drawn into the linear IOSurface the display scans out (~0.9 ms a full-screen layer,
session 9's probe). With the boot-arg, an IOSurface texture of a million pixels or more (the display's framebuffers),
one plane, BGRA8 or RGBA8, a render target not written by a kernel and not rotated, is a compressed texture of its own
that the passes draw into; the 2D engine lays it into the surface at the command buffer's commit, by the road a
framebuffer with a rotation already takes (-hakiosTurnInto:, hakios_metal_cb_blit_rotated with rotation 0, reading the
compressed copy). A pass that loads finds the last frame in the copy; the copy is filled from the surface first when it
is new or the processor wrote the surface since (its seed moved). A Clear or DontCare pass fills nothing. The first 24
such framebuffers are named ("compressed framebuffer N over IOSurface ..."): one per surface is the expected count,
one per frame would mean a fill every frame.

Lossless: the surface ends each command buffer with what was drawn, pixel for pixel. What it does not cover: another
texture object over the same surface read inside the same command buffer before the commit would see the frame before.

